 ## Overview
-Sort-first parallel rendering is a distributed volume rendering strategy where the viewport is decomposed into spatial tiles, and each process or node is responsible for rendering a specific part of the image rather than a part of the volume [S1][S2]. Unlike sort-last approaches that require a final compositing pass to merge results from volume partitions, sort-first rendering eliminates the need for compositing because each node renders its assigned image region directly to a local framebuffer [S1]. Sort-first partitioning is preferable to parallelize algorithms that depend on ray coherence, such as visibility culling or volumetric shadows [S2].
