 ### Parameters and Edge Cases
-*   **Load Balancing:** The system must dynamically reorganize the image decomposition based on per-pixel cost estimation [S2]. Static partitioning leads to inefficiencies.
+*   **Load Balancing:** The system must dynamically reorganize the image decomposition based on per-pixel cost estimation [S3, unofficial]. Static partitioning leads to inefficiencies [S3, unofficial].
 *   **Viewport Movement:** A significant pitfall is load balancing when moving the viewport. As the view changes, the data required for specific tiles may shift between processes, necessitating data movement between nodes [S1].
-*   **Data Scalability:** Sort-first decompositions can suffer from weak data scalability if the volume data is not partitioned efficiently. The bricking approach mitigates this [S2].
+*   **Data Scalability:** Sort-first decompositions can suffer from weak data scalability if the volume data is not partitioned efficiently. The bricking approach mitigates this [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The rendering time is dominated by the slowest node in the cluster. Dynamic load balancing attempts to minimize this by reorganizing the image decomposition based on per-pixel cost estimation [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Each node must store the volume bricks relevant to its assigned tiles. The bricking approach helps manage memory by only loading necessary data [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Eliminates the compositing bottleneck inherent in sort-last approaches [S1]. Sort-first partitioning is preferable to parallelize algorithms that depend on ray coherence, such as visibility culling or volumetric shadows [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Load balancing is challenging, especially during viewport navigation, due to the need to move data between processes [S1]. Data scalability can be an issue without proper bricking [S2].
-*   **When to Use:** Suitable for GPU-based cluster systems where compositing overhead is prohibitive and ray coherence can be exploited [S2].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Not ideal for scenarios with highly dynamic viewports where data movement overhead outweighs rendering benefits, or when the volume data cannot be effectively partitioned into bricks.
